CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a three-dimensional vector field plot drawn inside a light blue wireframe cube, with black x, y, and z axes running through it; x points toward the lower left, y to the right, and z vertically. A small shaded blue sphere sits where the axes cross, marked with the label 0, standing for a large mass at the origin. Arrows fill the interior of the cube and every one points inward toward that sphere. Those closest to the origin are long and bright orange, those at intermediate distance shorter and teal, and those near the corners and faces reduced to small dashes, so length and color together carry the magnitude of the field. Several of the longest arrows lie along the axis directions and meet the sphere head on. Because length and direction vary independently, the reader takes in both at once: the field aims at the central mass everywhere, and its strength climbs sharply as distance to the origin shrinks. The plot renders the gravitational field of a point mass, making visible the uniformly attractive radial direction and the rapid growth in magnitude near the source.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - light-blue wireframe cube centered on the origin (12 edges, no faces)
  - black x-axis with arrowhead pointing toward lower-left (toward viewer), italic label 'x'
  - black y-axis with arrowhead pointing right, italic label 'y'
  - black z-axis with arrowhead pointing up, italic label 'z'
  - small shaded blue sphere at the origin representing the point mass, with a highlight
  - label '0' just below-right of the sphere
  - 3D grid of vector arrows filling the cube interior, every arrow pointing radially inward toward the sphere
  - long bright-orange arrows in the inner shell nearest the origin, several lying exactly along the ±x, ±y, ±z axis directions and meeting the sphere head-on
  - medium teal arrows at intermediate radius
  - short dark teal/navy dashes near the cube faces and corners
  - a single highlighted sample point with its own field vector (driven by the radial-distance slider)
  - faint dashed radial line from origin through the sample point showing the radius r

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: cube is 4.4 × 4.4 × 4.4 units (spans −2.2 to +2.2 on each axis), centered at origin; axes are black lines spanning roughly −3 to +3 with cone arrowheads at the positive ends (and x extending visibly toward the viewer's lower-left, y to the right, z up). Sphere at origin: radius 0.24, light blue (#5B9BD5) with a specular highlight and a slightly darker rim; label '0' in black placed just below and right of the sphere. Axis labels italic serif 'x' at the lower-left end of x, 'y' at the right end of y, 'z' at the top of z. Cube wireframe color #2E9BE8, line width ~1.5, no fill. Field: F(r) = −G M r̂ / |r|², sampled on a 5×5×5 lattice of points at coordinates ±1.65, ±0.85, 0 (skip the origin cell, which is occupied by the sphere). Arrow tail placed at the sample point, pointing toward the origin; arrow length L = clamp(k/|r|², 0.12, 0.7) with k chosen so |r|≈0.85 gives L≈0.6 and |r|≈2.5 gives L≈0.12. Color by |r|: |r| < 1.1 → bright orange #F4761E; 1.1 ≤ |r| < 1.9 → teal #2FA6A0; |r| ≥ 1.9 → dark navy-teal #1B4F63 drawn as tiny dashes with small or no heads. Six of the orange arrows lie exactly on the ±x, ±y, ±z axes at distance 0.85 and terminate on the sphere surface. In 'Direction only' mode every arrow uses fixed length 0.33 and a single teal color #2FA6A0. Sample-point vector: drawn thicker (radius ~0.035 shaft), placed along the direction (0.55, 0.30, 0.78) normalized, at distance r from origin; its length uses the same 1/r² law but with a 1.6× multiplier so it is clearly the hero arrow, and a faint gray dashed segment runs from the origin to its tail with an 'r' label at the midpoint.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The arrows are a genuine 3D radial field: from any viewpoint every vector still aims at the central mass, and the inward pattern is spherically symmetric, not a flat rosette."
  },
  {
    "id": "sampleRadius",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Distance r of sample point from the mass",
    "range": [
      0.35,
      2.2,
      0.05
    ],
    "default": 1.2,
    "teaches": "As the sample point is dragged inward its vector still points at the origin but grows dramatically in length and shifts from dark teal to bright orange — magnitude scales like 1/r², direction never changes."
  },
  {
    "id": "fieldMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Field display",
    "options": [
      "Direction only",
      "Magnitude scaled"
    ],
    "default": "Magnitude scaled",
    "teaches": "Separating direction from strength: in direction-only mode every arrow is the same length and the pure inward (attractive) geometry is visible; in scaled mode the same directions carry the 1/r² magnitude as length and color."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The attractive direction",
    "narration": "Equal-length arrows throughout the cube reveal the underlying geometry: the gravitational field of a point mass points straight at the mass from every direction in space.",
    "state": {
      "fieldMode": "Direction only",
      "sampleRadius": 1.6
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Adding magnitude",
    "narration": "Switching to the scaled view keeps every direction identical but lets length and color carry strength — corner arrows shrink to dashes while the innermost arrows blaze orange.",
    "state": {
      "fieldMode": "Magnitude scaled",
      "sampleRadius": 1.6
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Closing in on the mass",
    "narration": "Drag the sample point inward: the vector stays aimed at the origin, but its length climbs sharply as r shrinks, showing the inverse-square growth near the source.",
    "state": {
      "fieldMode": "Magnitude scaled",
      "sampleRadius": 0.45
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Far from the mass",
    "narration": "Pushed out toward a cube corner, the same vector becomes a barely visible dark dash — the field weakens rapidly with distance while remaining perfectly radial.",
    "state": {
      "fieldMode": "Magnitude scaled",
      "sampleRadius": 2.1
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.62
}

NOTES: Build arrows as instanced cone+cylinder meshes or THREE.ArrowHelper instances grouped in one Object3D so the toggle can rescale all of them in place. Cube edges via THREE.LineSegments on an EdgesGeometry of a BoxGeometry with LineBasicMaterial (no depthWrite issues since nothing is opaque except the sphere). Axes as thin cylinders plus cone tips so they read correctly under orbit; render them with slight polygon offset so they stay visible where they pass through the arrow cloud. Labels x, y, z, 0 as sprite-based HTML/canvas textures that always face the camera. Interpolate arrow length/color smoothly (lerp over ~350 ms) when the mode toggle flips so the learner sees direction preserved while magnitude collapses. Use a white background, slight ambient + one directional light so only the sphere shades; arrows use MeshBasicMaterial for flat poster-like color matching the source. Keep OrbitControls damped, target at origin, zoom limited so the cube always fills ~60% of the frame.

Follow the interaction plan above.
